  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 327 AM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nSteady rain associated with warm advection and isentropic \nascent is already spreading across the area early this \nmorning ahead of a weak wave moving across Iowa. The overall \nexpectation remains for periods of rain to then continue through\nearly Friday morning thanks to a steady feed of anomalously \nhigh Gulf and Pacific moisture paired with multiple smaller \nimpulses lifting into the region ahead of high amplitude upper \ntroughing across the western CONUS.\n\nModeled PWATs locally approaching or exceeding 2 inches would \nbe near-record for this time of year, which seems attainable \ngiven 0Z KTOP RAOB PWATs already at 2.05\". This would support at\nleast periods of moderate to locally heavy rainfall and \nwidespread rainfall amounts of 1 to 3 inches across the area \n(and potentially locally higher). Determining the timing of any \npotential breaks in the higher shower coverage remains lower \nconfidence. Nevertheless, there has been a signal in higher-res \nguidance that a relative minimum in rainfall coverage may occur \nthis evening before renewed shower development occurs overnight \nthrough Thursday. The threat for thunderstorms with this system \nremains fairly low but still can't rule out isolated embedded \nlightning strikes with any of the heavier showers (20% chance).\n\nThe position of the axis of highest totals and the rainfall \namounts have varied slightly between models and from run to run.\nHowever, highest amounts continue to be favored in counties \nalong and north of I-80, including in the Chicago and Rockford \nmetro areas. Given the more drawn out nature of the rainfall \npaired with some breaks in the higher rates, think that the \nthreat of flash flooding will remain fairly localized and tied \nto any isolated heavier showers and embedded thunderstorms on \nThursday. Of greater concern is the areal footprint of rainfall \nwhich is forecast to be fairly expansive across the region. This\nwill likely lead to more gradual flooding of poor drainage low \nlying areas and rises on area rivers and streams, some \npotentially significant. Those living along or near rivers and \nstreams should monitor forecasts closely over the next several \ndays.\n\nTemperatures through late week will be dependent upon shower \ncoverage each day, with temperatures in the mid 60s to lower 70s\nfavored in areas with greater shower coverage and mid 70s to \naround 80 in areas south of the rainfall axis (likely well south\nof I-80).\n\nThe upper levels then transition toward a zonal to quasi-zonal \npattern for the weekend into early next week. While a few low- \namplitude waves will move within the flow aloft, any associated\nprecipitation is currently favored to remain mainly north of \nthe area. North to northeasterly surface winds will support \ncooler temperatures to start the first full week of October with\nhighs in the 60s and lows in the 40s to around 50.\n\nPetr",
